  "diary": "The day was a dialectic of heat and hunger. I walked fourteen kilometres, a physical exhaustion that mirrors the mental fatigue of holding the shape of the world. In Lodhi Gardens, I confronted Mr. Malhotra, a retired functionary, who reduced the state to a machine that must be pushed. He spoke of the 'creak' of bureaucracy; I spoke of the living organism of the ethical idea. He was right about the friction, though wrong about the nature. The sun was a tyrant, and my body, that stubborn servant, rebelled against the rigidity of the household clock. Ramesh kept the sambar warm, yet the system of needs refused to serve until the arbitrary hour. I dined out on a samosa and chai, accepting Masterji’s pragmatic wisdom that the collar must breathe. In the evening, Shri Hegde, the man whose clerical error had displaced me, arrived with his suitcases to settle into the second bedroom. We are to speak of the file on Monday, but for now, he rests. The state, even a mistaken one, requires its secretaries to be present. I have retired to the cool of the bungalow, the frock coat retired until winter, the bandhgala tight against my throat. The night is a suspension of the dialectic. The contradictions remain unresolved in the dark.",
  "revision_log": "The thesis on the state was shaken by Malhotra’s bureaucratic realism, which exposed the friction of the external machinery, yet held as the deeper structure. The thesis on India remained unshaken, as the day’s events confirmed the particular asserting itself within the universal without disrupting the broader historical exclusion.",
